<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bandung</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bandung is a city in the highlands of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a> province. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule it grew into a cool hill town with planned Art Deco districts and a technical college, and was intended as a future colonial capital. In January 1950 it was the scene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-westerling">Raymond Westerling</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> coup attempt, and in April 1955 it hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Asian–African Conference</a>, a forerunner of the Non-Aligned Movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bandung.jpg" alt="Bandung" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F1fans · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandung_View_dari_Gedung_Wisma_HSBC_Asia_Afrika_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bandung lies in a river basin on the western highland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, about 145 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, ringed by volcanic mountains rising to 2,400 metres. These mountains were the main reason the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> government planned to move its capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, now Jakarta, to the city. The basin's main river is the Citarum, and its branch the Cikapundung divides the city from north to south. The altitude gives it one of the coolest climates among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s provincial capitals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest reference to the area dates to 1488. In the 17th and 18th centuries the Dutch East Indies Company established plantations in the area, and in 1786 a supply road linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a>, Cianjur, Bandung, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumedang">Sumedang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a>. In 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ordered Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels">Herman Willem Daendels</a> to improve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>'s defences against a possible British invasion. Daendels had a road of about 1,000 km built across the island; laid in Bandung in 1810 as De Groote Postweg, the Great Post Road, it follows the present Jalan Asia-Afrika.</p><p>On his orders the regency's chief administrator, R. A. Wiranatakusumah II, moved his office to a site near a pair of holy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, now the city square, where he built a palace, a grand mosque and a pendopo in the classical Sundanese orientation. In 1856 the town became capital of the Preanger Regencies Residency, which it remained until 1925.</p><p>In the early 20th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> residents demanded a municipal government, which was granted in 1906, and the city developed into an exclusive resort for Europeans with hotels, cafés and shops. Braga Street, lined with boutiques selling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> fashions, gave the city the nickname Parijs van <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, the Paris of Java. In 1920 the architect Henri Maclaine Pont planned the first technical university in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, the Technische Hogeschool te Bandung, now the Bandung Institute of Technology. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> declared independence in 1945 it grew from a quiet town into a dense metropolis. In 1955 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> hosted the first Asian-African Conference there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bandung is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a> province. Most of its people are Sundanese, and Sundanese is widely spoken alongside standard Indonesian. The population was 2.444 million at the May 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is known for its music, including the angklung, and for its universities and creative industries, and it belongs to the UNESCO Creative Cities Network.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bandung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In April 1955 it hosted the Asian–African Conference at <b>Bandung</b>, where twenty-nine newly independent and emerging states met, a gathering that helped prepare the Non-Aligned Movement of 1961.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Members are expected to follow the ten <b>Bandung</b> principles of 1955, among them respect for human rights and the Charter of the United Nations, for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all nations, recognition of movements for national independence, the equality of all races and nations, non-intervention and…</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Living in Jakarta, he read an essay in Timbul magazine that challenged Indonesian musicians to compose a future national anthem, and he took up the challenge, finishing a song called &quot;Indonesia&quot; in <b>Bandung</b> in 1924.</blockquote><a href="/songs/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Indonesians saw the Dutch-created states as colonial puppets, and the attempted coup by Raymond Westerling's APRA forces in <b>Bandung</b> in January 1950 discredited federalism further.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-of-indonesia">United States of Indonesia</a></li></ul></section>
